course: dictionary
lesson: 7447
title: Pond: one small word, many places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pond is a small area of still water, smaller than a lake. Read the words around the word for clues. «A frog needs two habitats: its tadpoles live in water and breathe with gills, while adult frogs hunt insects on damp land.» The sentence tells you what a pond is for a frog: the place where its life begins.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Across the pond» is informal and means the Atlantic Ocean, not a small garden pond. Ask yourself: does the rest of the sentence fit a small pond, or something much bigger?
explain: Use the context to check safety too. «The advice of RoSPA is never to go onto ice on ponds, lakes, rivers or canals.» The list shows that ponds are one kind of water, and the warning tells you what to do near them.
fact: A pond is a small area of still water, smaller than a lake.
fact: «Pond» (informal) can mean the Atlantic Ocean, as in «across the pond».
fact: Pond life includes tadpoles, water snails and dragonflies.
fact: «Pond skaters» walk on the surface of the water, held up by surface tension, the tight pull between water molecules at the surface.
fact: Never go onto ice on a pond: it can break, and «people die every winter doing it».
exercise: What is a pond? | A huge area of salt water, like the sea | *A small area of still water, smaller than a lake | A stream of moving water | A river that rushes down a hill
exercise: «Water molecules pull tightly on each other at the surface; pond skaters walk on it.» What do pond skaters walk on? | *The surface of the water | Frost on the grass | The muddy bottom | Smooth stones on the bank
exercise: What does «across the pond» mean in informal English? | *The Atlantic Ocean | A short walk round the garden | The bottom of a lake | A pond in the park
test: Why is it dangerous to walk onto ice on a pond? | *The ice may break, and people die every winter doing it | Ice is always thicker than water | Ducks will be angry | It is too cold to walk on
test: Why does a frog need a pond? | Frogs cannot swim in rivers | Ponds keep frogs warm all winter | *Its tadpoles live in water, while adult frogs hunt on damp land | Frogs only eat pond weed
exam: Which of these would you find living in a pond? | Sharks and whales | Owls nesting in tall trees | Camels and penguins | *Tadpoles, water snails and dragonflies
